ital for a ct scan  on my lungs um i still  wasn't feeling well i was probably sat  there for about half an hour  until and then a member of staff came  over to me  because i was crying again because like  i was in a lot of pain and i was  obviously worried  and i got put into like a side ward into  a bed  and literally about five minutes later  two doctors and a consultant came over  to me  and then he said um basically  you have blood clots on your lungs and  we assume it's from the astrazeneca  vaccine  at this point i broke down in tears  because i was  really really worried um i'm only 32  i've got a four-year-old daughter and a  two-year-old son  and to be told that i had blood clots on  my lungs which  is deadly was scary and i did actually  ask him  am i gonna die and he said we need to  start  um treatment as soon as possible after  all these tests it come back  that um the blood clots  were in my leg if they they presume that  that's where they started  and then they traveled to my lungs  luckily there was no blood clots  on my brain you're still waiting to find  out if it's officially related to the  vaccine  but the doctors who you have seen so far  certainly suspect that on my uh  discharge notes it says vaccine induced  thrombosis and thrombocytopenia  every single doctor that i've seen has  said that they've never ever seen  this it's a kind of blood reaction  and clots and things in anyone you  should in theory be getting a second  astrazeneca dose  obviously you're not going to do that  right  no my um doctor advised me  not to get that because i would end up  in hospital again  it's not something that you ever think  that is going to happen to you  lauren thank you so much for sharing  your story with us and  glad you're feeling better and i hope it  improves much more thank you so much  thank you  and you can watch my full interview with  lauren on our website globalnews.ca  globalnational next the agonizing  anniversary in nova scotia as memories  of the mass killing  are still raw  it has been a year since a rural  community in nova scotia found itself in  the middle of a nightmare  a gunman disguised as an rcmp officer  killed 22 people  in a 13-hour rampage it began in the  tiny village of puerto pick  on april 18th and ended the next day  when the killer was finally found and  killed  by the police as a dreadful anniversary  approaches this weekend nova scotians  are still struggling  ross lord reports after a year of grief  and despair  some symbols of strength are emerging  for communities devastated by the mass  killings  like this steel monument created by a  man whose stepson  corey ellison was murdered along with 21  others  when i say in the poem you know you're  still in a part of our heart and  our mind you know we can go back to that  but we still have to live  and go on nearby there's a series of  memorial markers  honoring the victims and intended to  ease the pain of a murder spree too  horrific to ever be understood  i have a good friend who lost a very  close family member  and she's had a hard year it's senseless  senseless there's no re rhyme or reason  why somebody would  do that is in this day and age  in nova scotia healing collectively is  complicated  especially with pandemic restrictions  just ask those whose job it is to help  like pastor stephen adams those things  are really important and it's that  that the the ability to be able to  connect with other people  and it's been made a lot harder pastor  adams  says there's no shortage of goodwill i  know there are a lot of people  that care and a lot of people that want  to help and that's a good thing that's  always a good thing  for victims families including  schoolteacher lisa mccauley's sister  april 18th and 19th mark a dreaded first  anniversary it's agonizing  those dates trigger horrific memories we  are reliving the immediate pain that we  felt  jenny kirstad says therapy has helped  her cope but there are questions  about how the nova scotia mental health  system has responded in rural  communities  that were devastated by the mass  killings there's a fragmentation  i guess i would say it's fragmented and  that's really had implications i think  on the community and it will  moving forward as well serena lewis  lives in great village next to porter  pick  she just finished the term as nova  scotia's grief and bereavement  coordinator  she suggests traumatized communities  need a more concerted effort  but this is a specialized team of people  that are going to come to the  communities  meet people where they're at and try to  build this foundationally  from the ground up the need for support  is far from over  mental health counselors say for some  people the after effects of extreme  trauma can become even worse  in the second year there are new stop  gaps like nova scotia psychologists  offering three free sessions per person  to those  most affected i've received about a  dozen calls so far they're people who  didn't reach out originally  because they thought they were okay even  a year later these are early days  in the aftermath of something so hideous  and so shocking  ross laura global news debert nova  scotia  and that is global national for this  thursday i'm donna friesen tonight's  your canada is art rossen alberta  just east of edmonton thanks for  watching robin gill will be at the  anchor desk tomorrow and i'll see you on  saturday for the new reality  take care of yourselves bye-bye  [Applause]  you
 

